          %h2#idea_and_motivation Idea and Motivation
          %p
            One motivation for studying deformations over Artin rings is that one may wish to classify/parameterize or create a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of some particular type of object. Often it is very easy to write down a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+of+points"} functor of points
            for the space. For instance, curves of genus
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p
            But then one must ask, is this really a space? In other words, is it
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion"} completion
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
            at a point gives rise to the Artin rings we consider. The deformations of the object over infinitesimal thickenings gives us a way to tell what the points near our given point look like on the original
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
          %p
            This reduces us to now asking for a given deformation problem to be representable, but this is almost always too much to hope for as well. Schlessinger’s criterion refers to two weaker notions of representability, namely that of hulls and that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prorepresentable+functor"} prorepresentability
          %p Once we determine these formal local points, we must then go backwards and see if these correspond to actually points in the original space. This is known as effectivization.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            will denote a complete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noetherian+ring"} noetherian ring
          %p
            Unless otherwise specified, everything will be in the category of local Artin
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            and will be denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo
                      %mi Λ
                    %msub
                      %mi Art
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _\Lambda Art_k
          %p
            Define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo
                    %mi Λ
                  %msub
                    %mi Noeth
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} _\Lambda Noeth_k
            to be the category of complete noetherian local
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            A
            %em thickening
            is a surjection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A' \stackrel{f}{\to} A\to 0
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mi ker
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I=ker(f)
            satisfies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ′
                    %mi I
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_{A'}I=0
          %p
            A
            %em small thickening
            is a thickening in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is principal. Note that in our category any surjection can be factored as a sequence of small thickenings.
          %p
            A
            %em predeformation functor
            is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %msub
                    %mo :
                    %mi Λ
                  %msub
                    %mi Art
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: _\Lambda Art_k \to \text{Set}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(k)=\{*\}
            is a singleton. This condition is the notion that we are really over a “point”, and so any deformation must be the trivial one.
          %p
            The functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo ^
                  %msub
                    %mo :
                    %mi Λ
                  %msub
                    %mi Noeth
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{F}: _\Lambda Noeth_k\to \text{Set}
            is defined to be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mover
                        %mi F
                        %mo ^
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mi n
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔪
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \displaystyle \widehat{F}(A)= \lim_n F(R/\mathfrak{m}^n)
          %p
            A predeformation functor is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prorepresentable+functor"} prorepresentable
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{F}
            is representable.
          %p
            A hull for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R, \eta)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Λ
                  %msub
                    %mi Noeth
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\in _\Lambda Noeth_k
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo ∈
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta\in \widehat{F}(R)
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_R\to F
            is formally smooth and we have a bijection on tangent spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{h_R}\to T_F
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[\epsilon]
            is defined to be the ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[\epsilon]/(\epsilon^2)
            with the trivial
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
          %h2#the_conditions The Conditions
          %p
            Note that whenever we are given a predeformation functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'\to A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ″
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A''\to A
            we get an induced map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                  %mi A
                  %mo ″
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ″
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A'\times_A A'')\to F(A')\times_{F(A)} F(A'')
            by functoriality of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and the universal property of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %p
            Schlessinger gives four conditions in his
            %a{:href => "#Schless"} paper
            called (H1) - (H4).
          %p
            (H1) If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ″
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A''\to A
            is a small thickening then (*) is surjective.
          %p
            (H2) If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A=k
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ″
                    %mo =
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A''=k[\epsilon]
          %p
            (H3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_F
            is finite-dimensional
          %p
            (H4) If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ″
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A''=A'
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'\to A
            is a small thickening, then (*) is bijective
          %p Geometrically the conditions can informally be thought of as follows:
          %p One can think of (H1) as being able to “glue”.
          %p One can think of (H2) as gluing being unique over infinitesimal neighborhoods.
          %p (H3) is having a finite dimensional tangent space
          %p One can think of (H4) as gluing being unique on a small thickening over itself.
          %h2#deformation_functors Deformation Functors
          %p
            We call a predeformation functor a
            %em deformation functor
            if it satisfies (H1) and (H2). Here we make precise what was meant in the motivation section. Suppose you have a moduli functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo :
                  %mtext Sch
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{F}: \text{Sch}\to \text{Set}
            and you would like to know what the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            looks like in a neighborhood of some point say
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mover
                      %mi F
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mtext Spec
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_0\in \overline{F}(\text{Spec} k)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %msub
                    %mo :
                    %mi Λ
                  %msub
                    %mi Art
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: _\Lambda Art_k \to \text{Set}
            by the following prescription
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi η
                    %mo ∈
                    %mover
                      %mi F
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mtext Spec
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi η
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %mtext Spec
                        %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A)=\{ \eta \in \overline{F}(\text{Spec} A): \eta|_{\text{Spec} k}=\eta_0\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            can be seen to be a deformation functor.
          %p
            Of course, this doesn’t
            %em always
            work if we’re parametrizing objects with automorphisms, but this general approach does work for most of the examples in the motivation section and explains why this class of functors is what we consider.
          %h2#schlessingers_criterion Schlessinger’s Criterion
          %p
            The main theorem of Schlessinger’s
            %a{:href => "#Schless"} paper
            says that (H1), (H2), and (H3) are satisfied if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            has a hull. The second part of the theorem is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is prorepresentable if and only if in addition (H4) is satisfied.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Def
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Def_X
            be the predeformation functor parametrizing flat deformations of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is proper, then (H3) is satisfied and it has a hull.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Def
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Def_X
            is prorepresentable if and only if every automorphism extends over a small thickening.
          %p
            Consider the node
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %mtext Spec
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi xy
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=\text{Spec} k[ [ x,y ] ] /(xy)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ,
                  %mtext Spec
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi xy
                  %mo −
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k[ [ t ] ], \text{Spec} k [ [ x,y,t ] ] /(xy - t))
            is a hull for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Def
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Def_X
          %p
            %em Several more can be added.
          %h2#tangent_and_obstruction_theories Tangent and Obstruction Theories
          %p I haven’t come up with a nice succinct way to do this yet. I think there is a nice streamlined gerbe/stacky way to think about it in the Brian Osserman article cited below.
          %h2#effectivization Effectivization
          %p
            Given a deformation functor,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo ∈
                    %mover
                      %mi F
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta\in \widehat{F}(R)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext Spec
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Spec}R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R,\eta)
            is a hull for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            does there exist a universal object over
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext Spec
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Spec}R
          %p
            An example where we do have effectivization is Grothendieck’s existence theorem for coherent sheaves. Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mtext Spec
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to \text{Spec}A
            be proper and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            complete, local,
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noetherian+ring"} noetherian ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n=A/m^n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n=X\otimes_A A_n
            the thickenings over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℱ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_n
            is a compatible collection of coherent sheaves on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            whose restriction to each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℱ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}_n
          %h2#references References
          %p
            See also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deformation theory
            and references therein.
          %ul#Schless
            %li
              %p
                B. Osserman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Deformations and automorphisms: a framework for globalizing local tangent and obstruction spaces
            %li
              %p
                M. Schlessinger,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Functors of Artin rings
          %p this was a groundbreaking article at the time, still much cited.
